Temporomandibular Joint Disorder Systemic overlap
Complex Fatigue Entities
TMJ with widespread central sensitisation

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CFE-049
Reason for Visit: Urgent evaluation of intractable facial pain, severe headache, and inability to open the mouth to eat or drink.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported sudden, excruciating jaw pain resulting in lockjaw (trismus), accompanied by severe headache and extreme systemic distress. Immediate physical assessment was mandated to rule out acute dental abscess, temporal arteritis, or tetanus, and to administer potent muscle relaxants to break the pain-spasm cycle prior to standard hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My jaw is completely locked shut, the pain is shooting up into my temples, and my whole body aches from the stress.
History of Present Illness: Patient with known Fibromyalgia reports a severe acute exacerbation of localized temporomandibular joint pain. Over the last 12 hours, severe bruxism (teeth grinding) induced an extreme masseter muscle spasm, leading to severe jaw pain and marked trismus (inability to open the mouth fully). Patient notes the pain radiates into the temporal region and neck. The patient also describes widespread systemic body ache, confirming TMJ with widespread central sensitisation.
Functional Status: Highly distressed; unable to chew food or speak clearly due to restricted jaw movement.
Social/Safety Concerns: High anxiety regarding permanent jaw locking; risk of dehydration due to pain upon swallowing/opening mouth.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 mmHg | HR: 96 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 18 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Visibly tense, holding the side of the face, speaking through clenched teeth.
Physical Exam: Bilateral masseter and temporalis muscles are exquisitely tender to palpation and rock-hard (severe spasm). Max interincisal opening is severely restricted to approximately 15mm. No facial swelling, erythema, or fluctuance suggesting a dental abscess. Temporal arteries are non-tender and pulsatile. Neck exam shows severe trapezius spasm. Neurological exam of cranial nerves is otherwise normal.
Environmental Observations: Safe environment. Patient had been using ineffective cold packs.
Medication Reconciliation: Took Paracetamol and Ibuprofen safely with zero relief of the severe muscle spasm.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Temporomandibular Joint Disorder Systemic overlap - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Temporal Arteritis (Giant Cell Arteritis) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Pain is muscular and joint-based (jaw claudication usually occurs during chewing, not continuous locking spasm), and temporal arteries are normal on palpation.

Acute Dental Abscess - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Bilateral muscular presentation without focal gingival swelling, purulent discharge, or high fever.

Tetanus - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clear history of chronic bruxism/TMJ, up-to-date vaccination status, and absence of generalized descending tetany or risus sardonicus.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The sudden onset of severe trismus and facial pain required an urgent home visit to clinically exclude dangerous pathologies such as a deep space dental infection or giant cell arteritis. Having confirmed a severe, non-infectious masseter spasm overlapping with systemic central sensitization, immediate intervention with prescription muscle relaxants was required. Delaying this treatment would have left the patient in excruciating pain, unable to hydrate, and highly distressed by the locked jaw throughout the night.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered a single dose of oral Diazepam 5mg to rapidly break the masseter and temporalis muscle spasm. Prescribed Amitriptyline 10mg nocte for central pain modulation and to reduce nocturnal bruxism. Targeted intervention strategy includes Amitriptyline, Magnesium, and COX-2 + Cannabinoids.
Positive Behavioral Change: By continuously applying a warm, moist towel to the sides of your face for 20 minutes before attempting to eat, you will actively dilate the blood vessels and relax the masseter muscles, directly reducing the painful spasm and rewarding yourself with easier, pain-free jaw opening.
Home-Based Services Optional: Referral to a specialized TMJ physiotherapist and dentist.
DME/Safety Optional: Recommended obtaining a custom-fitted occlusal splint (night guard).
Follow-up: Routine GP and Dental review in 1 week to optimize chronic management and splinting.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department via 000 if patient develops sudden loss of vision, if the jaw becomes physically dislocated and sticks open, or if severe swelling develops under the chin/neck causing breathing difficulty.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Severe muscle spasms around the jaw mimic structural joint damage; long-term resolution requires specialized dental appliances to prevent grinding.
Clinical Course: The muscle relaxant will significantly reduce the jaw locking within 1-2 hours. The surrounding facial and neck soreness will take several days to fully resolve.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call 000 immediately if you suddenly lose your vision, or if your jaw pops completely out of place and you cannot close your mouth.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The muscles controlling your jaw have gone into a severe, locked cramp due to intense grinding; the medication acts as a specific chemical relaxer to force those muscles to let go.
Actionable Steps: Strictly stick to a soft-food, no-chew diet (soups, smoothies) for the next 48 hours; resting the jaw joints completely prevents secondary inflammation of the joint capsule, positively reinforcing the medication's relaxing effect.

